- Use DOMPurify.sanitize() before passing to dangerouslySetInnerHTML:
    import DOMPurify from 'isomorphic-dompurify';
    <div dangerouslySetInnerHTML={{ __html: DOMPurify.sanitize(userContent) }} />
- Configure DOMPurify for React applications:
    import DOMPurify from 'isomorphic-dompurify';
    const cleanHtml = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirty, {
      USE_PROFILES: { html: true },
      FORBID_TAGS: ['style', 'script'],
      FORBID_ATTR: ['onerror', 'onload', 'onclick']
    });
- For Next.js SSR, use isomorphic-dompurify:
    npm install isomorphic-dompurify
    import DOMPurify from 'isomorphic-dompurify';
- Use @next/third-parties or react-html-parser with sanitization
- Prefer React's default JSX rendering which auto-escapes:
    <div>{userContent}</div>  // Safe: auto-escaped by React
- For markdown rendering, use rehype-sanitize:
    import rehypeSanitize from 'rehype-sanitize';
    <ReactMarkdown rehypePlugins={[rehypeSanitize]}>{content}</ReactMarkdown>
- Deploy Content-Security-Policy headers:
    Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'nonce-{random}' 'strict-dynamic'; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; object-src 'none';
- Never use ref.current.innerHTML as a workaround for dangerouslySetInnerHTML
